cuban numbers c_n = (n+1)^3 - n^3 = 3 n (n+1) + 1 = 6 T_n + 1 that are of Pierpont form 2^i 3^j + 1:
  c_n - 1 = 3 n (n+1) is 3-smooth iff n and n+1 are both 3-smooth iff n in {1, 2, 3, 8}
  (consecutive 3-smooth numbers: Levi ben Gerson 1343; checked here for n < 10^6).
  n with 3n(n+1) 3-smooth, n < 10^6: [1, 2, 3, 8]
    n = 1: 6 T_n = 6 = 2^1 3^1,  c_n = 7 = {7: 1}   PRIME
    n = 2: 6 T_n = 18 = 2^1 3^2,  c_n = 19 = {19: 1}   PRIME
    n = 3: 6 T_n = 36 = 2^2 3^2,  c_n = 37 = {37: 1}   PRIME
    n = 8: 6 T_n = 216 = 2^3 3^3,  c_n = 217 = {7: 1, 31: 1}   composite
  so the cuban primes that are Pierpont primes are exactly 7, 19, 37 -- and
  A(2,2) = 1 + 2 [7 prime] + 3 [19 prime] + 2 [37 prime] = 8  (see q41_fibre_count.log).
  7 + 19 + 37 = 4^3 - 1 = 63 and 1 + 7 + 19 + 37 = 64 (telescoping); 36 = T_3^2 = 6 T_3 because T_3 = 6.

done in 1 s
